Subject: [PATCH] MIDI In: honour a refused port, and let one be reserved for another app

MIDI inputs are exclusive on Windows: midiInOpen fails if another process
already holds the device, and RtMidi surfaces that through the live IsOk/Error
pair rather than a return value. MidiIn.OpenPort is void, and TryOpenPorts /
PollNewPorts registered the port no matter what came back:

    port.OpenPort(i, name);
    _ports.Add(port);
    _portNames.Add(name);

A port held by, say, a VJ application running alongside the editor therefore
showed up as connected, never delivered a message, and was never retried --
PollNewPorts skips any name already in _portNames. The banner stayed green, and
Reconnect only helped if the other application happened to let go in between.
Diagnosed on a show machine where the editor and MadMapper share two
controllers; the symptom reads as "the controller is dead".

Honouring the refusal:

 - check IsOk after OpenPort, on In and on Out. A refused In is logged once with
   the driver's own message, disposed, and left out of _ports/_portNames, so the
   retry keeps trying and IsConnected tells the truth;
 - keep the refused ports in _busyPorts, exposed as BusyPortNames /
   AnyPortBusy / BusyPortError, and show them in the MIDI Map banner: that is
   the one case where "no controller detected" is the wrong thing to say;
 - scan every port index in PollNewPorts instead of starting at _ports.Count: a
   refused port is not in _ports, so the old loop could never look at it again
   (names already open are still skipped, as before);
 - throttle TryOpenPorts to the same 3 s cadence as the poll. It now stays
   subscribed for as long as a port is missing or busy, and probing the driver
   on every editor tick would cost frames in a focused editor. Reconnect primes
   the timer so it still re-opens on the very next tick.

Arbitrating the conflict, rather than only surviving it -- MidiHub opens every
input it can see, so on a shared machine it takes controllers that belong to the
other application:

 - SetPortIgnored(name, bool) / IsPortIgnored, persisted editor-wide under
   MidiHub_IgnoredPorts. An ignored input is never opened, and is closed on the
   spot if it was open, so the other application can take it without waiting;
   clearing the flag primes the retry and takes it back on the next tick;
 - AvailablePortNames lists every input the driver exposes, open or not, so a
   port can be reserved before Kontrols ever gets to it. It is refreshed by
   ReadPortNames, one probe pass now shared by both open paths instead of two
   MidiIn.Create() per index;
 - a fold-out under the MIDI Map banner, one line per input: a toggle to listen
   or leave it alone, and its state (listening / busy / ignored);
 - ClosePort(name) closes a single In and the feedback Out that came with it;
   _outPortNames keeps that pairing, which nothing tracked before.

Recovery is now automatic: free the controller in the other application and
Kontrols takes the port back within 3 s, no Reconnect, no domain reload. And a
controller meant for that application can be kept out of Kontrols' reach for
good.

Package: com.blindspot.kontrols 0.5.1 - Unity 6000.3.10f1
Rationale, decisions and known limits: see RAPPORT.md shipped alongside.
